<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Andros</h1><p class="st-meta">1207 CE – 1566 CE (approx.) · capital Andros</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Latin lordship on the Cycladic island of Andros, a vassal of the Duchy of the Archipelago that survived until the Ottoman takeover in 1566.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1207 the Venetian Marino Dandolo, a companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-sanudo">Marco Sanudo</a>, conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/andros">Andros</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and held it as a fief under Sanudo's <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-the-archipelago">Duchy of the Archipelago</a>. The island was fertile and well placed on the sea route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> and the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A small Italian elite held the castle and the best land, while Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> islanders produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, wine and grain. The lordship passed among Venetian families, including the Ghisi, Sanudo and Zeno, and was disputed in dynastic quarrels with the dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/naxos">Naxos</a>. Pietro Zeno held it from 1384, and from about 1440 the Sommaripa family, starting with Crusino I, ruled it for most of its remaining history.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Increasing Turkish raids left <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/andros">Andros</a> dependent on Venetian protection and Ottoman tolerance. In 1566, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> removed the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crispo-family">Crispo</a> duke of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/naxos">Naxos</a>, they also ended Sommaripa rule on Andros, which was annexed to the empire.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1207 CE – 1566 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.72%;width:7.14%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1207 CE – 1566 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Andros</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Marino Dandolo, Crusino I Sommaripa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Eastern Orthodox Christianity (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian, Medieval Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Andros (Latin)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1386&amp;focus=lordship-of-andros" aria-label="Lordship of Andros on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5302 817 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Andros lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5622 1073L5636 1097M5622 1073L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5636 1097h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5622 1073h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5622 1073h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Andros. <a href="/atlas?year=1386&amp;focus=lordship-of-andros">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

